#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097681} :activity/id #uuid "8b72f1bb-5af6-54d1-9908-d6fc5cdc77fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049953 :topic/id #uuid "20ec2a7d-e711-5ee0-a76f-c2c6805b5674" :topic/math-academy-id 1146} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097886 :tutorial/content "Given a sphere with radius $r= 3\\:\\textrm{cm},$ how do we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/8d/8df0347cb30ac58b9cbcef4b7a7e25dfd35c899b1c833cdf2e8e22bcfc51971f.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV= \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=3\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\cdot (3)^3\n=36 \\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi.$ However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation. In this case, we get\n\n$$\nV \\approx 113.10 \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "d15351c6-0d8b-5a7e-86b4-509daefe7cf5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5394 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7ce10134-fed4-568d-af3e-a51e49129b8a" :step/math-academy-id 33643 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096588 :question/id #uuid "012ce1ef-e254-5e9c-992b-e32d1e7c7043" :question/math-academy-id "e-9000"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15868ae6-7843-5ae2-9fce-4d7b662f6763" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555814}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Sphere Given Its Radius or Diameter"} :step/id #uuid "c0a1895e-c996-52e0-b8b8